 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Family",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Four grades of one half-duplex RS-485 transceiver, differing in temperature range and some limits: SN55LBC176 -55 °C to 125 °C (JG ceramic DIP, FK 20-pad chip carrier), SN65LBC176 -40 °C to 85 °C (D SOIC, P PDIP), SN65LBC176Q -40 °C to 125 °C (automotive, D SOIC), SN75LBC176 0 °C to 70 °C (D SOIC, P PDIP). All run from one 5 V supply. The switching tables give SN55LBC176 and SN65LBC176Q one set of limits and SN65LBC176 and SN75LBC176 another."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Standards",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Within its specified conditions and limits, the part satisfies TIA/EIA-485-A as well as ISO 8482:1987(E). Across the full military temperature range a few of those limits fall short or go untested, and the driver meets the standard's VOD figures only above 0 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Driver and receiver",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "One three-state differential driver and one differential receiver. The driver output pair and receiver input pair are joined inside to form the A/B bus port, which loads the bus lightly when the driver is off or VCC is 0. The port has wide positive and negative common-mode ranges (–7 V to 12 V recommended), suiting party-line buses."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Enables and direction",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "DE enables the driver when high; RE enables the receiver when low. Tying DE and RE together gives one direction-control line. With both driver and receiver off, supply current falls to 200 µA maximum (250 µA for SN55LBC176 and SN65LBC176Q)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Receiver thresholds and failsafe",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "The receiver output is high for VID of 0.2 V or more and low for –0.2 V or less; between those it is indeterminate. Hysteresis is 50 mV typical. With the bus inputs open the output is high (open-circuit failsafe)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Protection",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The driver limits its output current in both directions and the device has thermal shutdown. The bus sees no glitch while the supply ramps up or down."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input and output structures",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Each bus terminal carries a nominal 18 kΩ path plus 100 kΩ nominal, to VCC on A and to ground on B. Inside the A/B port a nominal 3 kΩ resistor runs to VCC and a nominal 1.1 kΩ resistor in series with two diodes runs to ground. The receiver output is a complementary MOS stage. D, RE and DE share one input structure."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal metrics",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "θJA depends heavily on the board (about 50%), on altitude (about 20%) and on device power (about 5%), so it compares packages under defined conditions and is a poor predictor of junction temperature in a given installation. The low-K JEDEC board has one 25 mm signal trace layer of 2 oz copper; its high-K counterpart adds two buried 1 oz planes. The two boards give θJA differing by 4% to 50%. θJC is measured with the package against a copper cold plate and suits heatsink use; θJB is junction to board centre with the board held in a cold-plate clamp, defined only on the high-K board, and suits one-dimensional thermal network models."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Handling",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "The device can be damaged by electrostatic discharge; handle it with ESD precautions."
  }
 ],
